 ### [Levenberg-Marquardt Pose Optimization](11301-levenberg-marquardt-pose-optimization.md)
-Levenberg-Marquardt (LM) Pose Optimization is an iterative numerical method that estimates the 6-DoF camera pose by minimizing the squared error between features extracted from a reference image and features rendered from a current pose guess. It distinguishes itself from gradient-based differentiable rendering approaches by relying on a numerically estimated Jacobian via finite-difference pose perturbations rather than analytical gradients, making it robust to complex lens and illumination dependencies.
+Levenberg-Marquardt (LM) Pose Optimization is an iterative numerical method that estimates the 6-DoF camera pose by minimizing the squared error between features extracted from a reference image and features rendered from a current pose guess. It distinguishes itself from gradient-based differentiable rendering approaches by relying on a numerically estimated Jacobian via finite-difference pose perturbations rather than analytical gradients.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The method runs for a fixed number of iterations `num_iter`. Each iteration involves a forward pass through the differentiable renderer and a backward pass. The complexity is $O(\text{num\_iter} \cdot (N_{\text{render}} + N_{\text{grad}}))$, where $N_{\text{render}}$ is the cost of rendering and $N_{\text{grad}}$ is the cost of backpropagation [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** The memory usage is dominated by the scene representation (e.g., 3D Gaussians or NeRF weights) and the intermediate activations during the forward and backward passes. The pose optimization itself adds minimal overhead ($O(1)$ for the pose tensors) [G].
-- **Measured Performance:** The specific performance metrics (PSNR, SSIM) depend on the scene and the initial pose accuracy. The method is designed to refine poses to improve rendering quality, particularly in test sets where poses might be less accurate or unavailable [S1, unofficial].
